<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gregory Chioniades</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1240–c. 1320</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory Chioniades was a Byzantine astronomer who travelled around 1295–1300 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> to study <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a> at the court of the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhans</a>. He translated Persian astronomical tables and texts into Greek, introducing Islamic astronomy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. He later served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Tabriz before returning to Trebizond.</p></div>
